; 05-const.kurt

; Every symbol is either a *constant* -- a fixed, specific object -- or a
; *variable* -- a stand-in for arbitrary objects (see 06-var.kurt).  `const`
; declares a symbol as a constant explicitly:

const zero
const one, two          ; comma-separated, just like `bool`

; Once a symbol's role is fixed on a level, it stays fixed: you cannot turn a
; constant into a variable afterwards.
;
;     var zero          ; would fail: `zero` is already used as a constant

; You rarely need `const` directly at the top level -- most of the time a
; constant gets introduced implicitly, e.g. by `def` (lesson 11) or `let`
; (lesson 21), simply by naming one particular object.
